        "thinking": "Now I understand the full picture. The workflows (`heartbeat.ts` and `plugin-dispatch.ts`) currently call the sandbox bridge via HTTP using KV-stored URLs. The `container.ts` executor calls `http://container/execute`, which matches what a CF Container binding would provide.\n\nHowever, there's an important architectural distinction:\n- The sandbox bridge handles agent heartbeat execution (running the actual LLM agent logic and adapter execution) \n- Plugin dispatch handles plugin job execution\n\nThe heartbeat workflow calls `/internal/heartbeat` on the bridge — this is NOT the same as `/execute`. This seems to be calling the heartbeat logic of the sidecar/companion server, not a command executor.\n\n\nThe container executor provides the command execution interface (`POST /execute` and `POST /cancel/:runId`) that adapters use to run commands inside the sandbox, while the workflows are actually calling higher-level endpoints on the sidecar that orchestrate the full heartbeat and plugin job lifecycle rather than just executing individual commands. are all internal API endpoints on the sidecar/companion server, NOT raw command executor endpoints.\n\nSo there's a fundamental distinction:\n- The container.ts executor (`ContainerExecutor`) is for running commands via `POST /execute` (like `ssh`, running shell commands)\n- The heartbeat/plugin-dispatch workflows call higher-level internal API endpoints on the sidecar\n\nFor the heartbeat to be fully CF-native, we'd need the agent to run inside a CF Container that exposes `/internal/heartbeat`. That container would include the full Node.js server logic.\n\nFor plugin-dispatch to be CF-native, the container would need to expose `/internal/plugin-jobs/:id/claim`, `/execute`, `/complete`.\n\nThis is more complex than just wiring up `ContainerExecutor`. The workflows are calling HTTP endpoints that represent high-level operations, not just command execution.\n\nBut I think what the user really means by \"ensure the entire system to be cloudflare native\" is that instead of needing an external sandbox bridge service running elsewhere, all execution should happen within Cloudflare's infrastructure via CF Containers. The approach would involve adding container bindings to `wrangler.toml` and the `Env` interface, then updating the workflows to use those bindings when in container mode. However, this only solves the command executor interface—the plugin job lifecycle endpoints like `/internal/heartbeat` and `/internal/plugin-jobs/:id/claim` are higher-level operations that aren't the same as raw command execution. The sidecar currently runs as an external Node.js server, but to make it CF-native, I'd deploy it as a CF Container and have the workflows communicate with it through a service binding instead of making HTTP calls to an external bridge URL. This means updating `wrangler.toml` to define the container binding and then modifying the workflow code to use `this.env.SIDECAR_SERVICE.fetch(...)` for those high-level operations.\n\nConfiguring the container binding... For `plugin-dispatch.ts`, I need to make the same updates—swap in the `SIDECAR_SERVICE` fetcher and potentially use a separate `PLUGIN_CONTAINER` binding when running in container mode. Then I should clean up stale comments in `api.ts`. I'm also realizing that for backward compatibility with non-Cloudflare deployments, I should keep `SIDECAR_URL` and `SIDECAR_API_KEY` as optional fallbacks in `env.ts`, preferring the `Fetcher` binding when available. Now I need to trace through where `SIDECAR_URL` is actually being used in the codebase to understand what needs to change if we switch to the Fetcher binding approach.",
